.SS pdf2iso
.nf
usage: py-pdftools pdf2iso [-h] [-o OUTPUT | -d OUTDIR]
                           [-t {a-1b,a-2b,a-3b,x-4}] [-p PASSWORD]
                           [--suffix SUFFIX] [--icc ICC]
                           [--condition CONDITION] [--validate] [--light]
                           [--verapdf VERAPDF] [--version] [-v]
                           inputs [inputs ...]

Convert PDF files to PDF/A or PDF/X-4 using Ghostscript and pikepdf.

positional arguments:
  inputs                PDF file(s) to convert

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  -o, --output OUTPUT   output file (only with a single input)
  -d, --outdir OUTDIR   directory for outputs (default: next to each input)
  -t, --target {a-1b,a-2b,a-3b,x-4}
                        standard to convert to (default: a-2b)
  -p, --password PASSWORD
                        password for encrypted inputs
  --suffix SUFFIX       suffix added to output names (default: _pdfa or
                        _pdfx)
  --icc ICC             ICC profile for the output intent: sRGB for PDF/A,
                        the printer's CMYK profile for PDF/X (default:
                        Ghostscript's own profiles)
  --condition CONDITION
                        PDF/X only: registered name of the printing
                        condition the --icc profile describes, e.g. FOGRA39,
                        FOGRA51, CGATS TR 006 (default: Custom)
  --validate            PDF/A only: validate with veraPDF and fail if it is
                        missing (PDF/X-4 output is always given a built-in
                        structural check)
  --light               PDF/A-2b and 3b only: first try a light conversion
                        that keeps the pages as they are (pikepdf only,
                        without Ghostscript), and keep it if veraPDF passes
                        it; otherwise convert with Ghostscript. Useful for
                        scans that Ghostscript makes much larger. Needs
                        veraPDF
  --verapdf VERAPDF     path to the veraPDF command-line tool
  --version             show program's version number and exit
  -v, --verbose         show Ghostscript and validator output
.fi
.SS ocr
.nf
usage: py-pdftools ocr [-h] [-l LANGUAGES] [--pages PAGES] [--rotate]
                       [-o FILE | -i | --output-dir FOLDER] [--report]
                       [--doubtful CONFIDENCE] [--password PASSWORD]
                       [--languages] [--download NAME [NAME ...]]
                       [PDF ...]

Read the text of the scanned pages of PDFs with tesseract, and write it
under their pictures, invisible, so that it can be found, copied and marked.
Pages that have text already are left as they are. Each PDF is written as
NAME-ocr.pdf beside it, unless -o, -i or --output-dir say otherwise.

positional arguments:
  PDF                   the PDFs to read

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  -l, --lang LANGUAGES  the languages of the text, as tesseract names them,
                        several with +, the main one first: lat+spa (by
                        default, those of the settings)
  --pages PAGES         the pages to read: 1-5,9, LAST ... (by default, all
                        the scanned pages)
  --rotate              turn the pages lying sideways or upside down before
                        reading them (needs tesseract's osd model:
                        --download osd)
  -o, --output FILE     the PDF to write (one input only)
  -i, --in-place        write each PDF over itself
  --output-dir FOLDER   write the PDFs into FOLDER, with the same names
  --report              list the doubtful words (page, confidence, word), to
                        be checked
  --doubtful CONFIDENCE
                        words read with a confidence below this (0 to 100)
                        are doubtful (default: 60)
  --password PASSWORD   the password of the PDFs, if they have one
  --languages           show tesseract's version and its languages here (★:
                        a better model), and stop
  --download NAME [NAME ...]
                        download tesseract's most accurate models of these
                        languages (and osd, to turn pages) into py-pdftools'
                        own folder, and stop
.fi
.SS combine
.nf
usage: py-pdftools combine [-h] -o FILE [--bookmarks {files,keep,none}]
                           [--labels] [--password PASSWORD]
                           FILE [FILE ...]

Combine PDFs into one: their pages one file after another, with their links,
annotations and form fields. FILE:PAGES takes only those pages, in that
order (book.pdf:1-3,7).

positional arguments:
  FILE                  a PDF, or PDF:PAGES

options:
