                              [--case-sensitive] [--ignore-accents]
                              [--no-dehyphenate] [--show-logical]
                              [--detailed] [--context] [--context-width N]
                              [--sort] [--txt FILE] [--csv FILE]
                              [--password PASSWORD]
                              FILE [TERM ...]

Find words, phrases or patterns in a PDF, and give, on a line for each term,
the labelled (printed) pages where it appears: lines ready for an index.

positional arguments:
  FILE                 the PDF
  TERM                 the words or phrases to find; a term that begins with
                       're:' is a regular expression

options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
  --words-file FILE    a file with a term on each line ('#' begins a
                       comment)
  --regex              every term is a regular expression (or put 're:'
                       before each)
  --regex-word         regular expressions, too, only match whole words
  --substring          find the terms inside words too (by default, only
                       whole words)
  --case-sensitive     match case
  --ignore-accents     ignore accents
  --no-dehyphenate     don't join the words split by a hyphen at the end of
                       a line
  --show-logical       also give the sequential page (its place in the
                       file), in brackets, where it differs from the label
  --detailed           a detailed report: how many times each term appears,
                       and a page on each line
  --context            a fragment of text around each place (implies
                       --detailed)
  --context-width N    characters of context on each side (default: 60)
  --sort               the list in alphabetical order (by default, in the
                       order of the terms)
  --txt FILE           also write the list, in alphabetical order, to a text
                       file
  --csv FILE           also write the results to a CSV file
  --password PASSWORD  the password to open the PDF

For instance: py-pdftools find-words book.pdf amor "re:virtu(s|tem|tis)"
--ignore-accents --context
.fi
.SS completion
.nf
usage: py-pdftools completion [-h] [--interface {en,es}] SHELL

Print the script that completes py-pdftools's commands, options and files in
SHELL. For instance: py-pdftools completion fish >
~/.config/fish/completions/py-pdftools.fish

positional arguments:
  SHELL                bash, fish or zsh

options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
.fi
.SS interface
.nf
usage: py-pdftools interface [-h] [--interface {en,es}] [{en,es}]

The language of the help, the messages and the window. The choice is kept in
the configuration file; --interface CODE changes it for one run only.

positional arguments:
  {en,es}              the language to use from now on; without it, the
                       languages are listed and the one in use is marked

options:
  -h, --help           show this help message and exit
  --interface {en,es}  the language of the interface, for this run only (to
                       keep a choice, use the 'interface' command)
.fi
.SH FILES
The configuration file, with the language of the interface and the settings: ~/.config/py-pdftools/config.toml (or $PY_PDFTOOLS_CONFIG/config.toml).
.SH SEE ALSO
The window, which also annotates and redacts documents: py-pdftools-gui [file.pdf].
